  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>The duskyfin glass fish, Parambassis wolffii, is a large, predatory ambassid from Thailand, Laos, Cambodia, Vietnam and Indonesia. It inhabits slow-moving and standing waters, including floodplains, and is very common in its native countries, where it is used as a food fish. It grows to around 20 cm, though not usually in aquaria, and eats any small tankmates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>Cambodia, Thailand, Laos, Vietnam, Indonesia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits slow-moving and standing waters, including floodplains.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 20 cm SL. Aquarium base: 120 × 30 cm. Volume: ~108 litres (120 × 30 × 30 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>A dark substrate will help to make this shy species less nervous and will also encourage it to display its best colours. Provide cover by planting some areas of the tank densely, along with some floating vegetation. Rocks and driftwood can also be used. This is a freshwater species and does not require the addition of salt to its tank.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 18–25°C</li><li>pH: 6.5–8.0</li><li>Hardness: 8–20 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>This species is more predatory than other commonly imported Parambassis species. Although it will usually accept dried foods, these should not form the basis of the diet. Offer live foods such as small earthworms, bloodworm etc. Frozen prawns, bloodworm and brine shrimp should also be provided.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>P. wolffii is predatory by nature and any small tankmates will be eaten. It is best kept with larger species such as gouramis, medium-sized barbs, rainbowfish etc. It is a shoaling species and does best when kept in a group of at least 6.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>Unknown. Adult females will most likely be fuller in the belly when in breeding condition.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>Not thought to have been bred in captivity, although the process is probably similar to that of Chanda ranga.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/parambassis-wolffii",
      "html": "<p>This species is an infrequent import. It is very common in its native countries, where it is used as a food fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
